#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125528} :activity/id #uuid "76681192-0bc8-5fd9-98fc-5fd254bc93fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049255 :topic/id #uuid "e711f285-9c99-50d8-8dee-a4a8b01a7c1a" :topic/math-academy-id 1936} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536170} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536169}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "2f62c8fc-daae-562a-a01e-851a375ec7a4" :step/math-academy-id 12678 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125749 :tutorial/content "We can consider $f(\\mathbf{r}(t))$ as the values taken by the function $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$ So, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))]\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the **rate of change of $f$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$**\n\nFurthermore, if $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is a level curve $f(x,y)=c,$ then by the chain rule we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =c \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(f(r(t))) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(c) \\\\ ∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can conclude that the gradient vector $\\nabla f$ is orthogonal to every level curve of $f.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "06350cc4-9c76-53db-a464-43e93f2fcc2f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2652 :tutorial/title "The Derivative as a Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "ea045ecb-90e1-5dc8-804d-711207e5f86c" :step/math-academy-id 12681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124277 :question/id #uuid "033e459e-5148-5e78-ab5c-7e455b06cd64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3695"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485588}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"} :step/id #uuid "ef0f7fd7-782e-55cb-8008-f28d770450ed" :step/math-academy-id 12682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124278 :question/id #uuid "f44b6ea6-694c-5f54-94ee-4c4a699bf4eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3696"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479889}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "cde1eff3-797d-5f65-a385-6296a6f32db3" :step/math-academy-id 12684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125835 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the function \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{r}) = f(x,y) = xy^2,\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t) \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nas input. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right]?$\n\nTo do this, we use the **multivariable chain rule in vector form**, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now apply this to our functions. First, we compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(f(x,y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(f(x,y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{2},2xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2},2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =-{sin}^{3}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "673ff670-99f1-5416-aa83-5c4c8bf0e4f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4967 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "87b55278-4bb6-5686-b4ef-178d5036b236" :step/math-academy-id 30935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124404 :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479902}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "4850b0cc-54ab-5a26-9440-b0d1cf757680" :step/math-academy-id 30936 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125836 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for a function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x_1(t), x_2(t), \\ldots, x_m(t) \\right\\rangle$ is a vector-valued function of $m$ variables, is the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nHow does this chain rule in vector form relate to the chain rule we have seen before, which states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d f} {\\textrm d t} \t= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_1}{\\textrm d t}  \n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_2}{\\textrm d t} + \\dots + \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_m}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet's expand $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)$ to see. Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))=⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{\\text{d}t}+⋯+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey are exactly the same, just different notations!\n\n**Note**: If we replace $\\mathbf{r}$ with a single-valued function $g(t),$ we get back the chain rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(g(t))] & =∇f(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "15062cce-eede-545a-ae5d-e02432a0ba33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4968 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Vector Form of the Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "fd19c878-fabf-5b99-9507-9a32fb862324" :step/math-academy-id 30937}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124728}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485631} :activity/id #uuid "0493dc8c-3f2a-5f14-bb7c-04f41f578eb4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049255 :topic/id #uuid "e711f285-9c99-50d8-8dee-a4a8b01a7c1a" :topic/math-academy-id 1936} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b55c69-c88a-5b54-9cd7-12d2acc90eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358067} {:answer/id #uuid "46655cc2-46c6-57d9-aae7-530ec487a9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358354} {:answer/id #uuid "5de3fd27-1a74-5e78-9c11-0753f0b2af77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358573} {:answer/id #uuid "97ab5991-5eae-5b87-9b48-34164ebe108a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359079} {:answer/id #uuid "a69903fd-123e-53f6-9cf8-6199c101e2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359079} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8f9bfb-8eb2-5607-8ba8-b9429f52c201" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "530f5767-4f31-5ef7-a6dd-bdeac420a724" :question/math-academy-id "q-94891" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-1,3,2t⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t-1,$ $y=3$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(3)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(2t)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 1+\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 0+\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2+8t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =\\frac{10(0)-2}{5(0)^{2}-2(0)+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2989f12e-e4d2-5eea-a076-313f05f3b144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336ae663-8acc-530e-96cc-0c9939efc8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-3{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358206} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8323a2-f87f-54c1-ba41-37494660f68d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358429} {:answer/id #uuid "68b9473e-f106-5375-ac81-7275f6b0a9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358670} {:answer/id #uuid "734c0594-8b9d-5fed-bb61-015770db6c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358754} {:answer/id #uuid "f34de6af-084c-5ec3-8d75-77cbd5aa09e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358670} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e56fa10-b537-572f-8386-98aeb21d532f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97fd3ba8-8ab5-5d07-adaa-98357dd8d4e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94873" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{-t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{-t}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j)\\cdot ((-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})+2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =2{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3232ec86-62d5-5c61-ba3f-93622480e150" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5305687e-83d8-5c87-af7d-a5b0a362d788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t+{e}^{{t}^{2}}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358466} {:answer/id #uuid "631de459-4352-5ad1-949e-8ac23d4b20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358613} {:answer/id #uuid "b15809fa-40bc-5563-9a51-d40e84efc938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359292} {:answer/id #uuid "c66b80bb-3662-52ab-bf2e-dab3fb4673d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{e}^{{t}^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359496} {:answer/id #uuid "e0073e63-c4b1-5f0a-b806-ed90e183c49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+\\frac{{e}^{{t}^{2}}}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359292} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86a4006-7e81-5ee9-a91c-4ead86d71714" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "312e3468-92a5-5996-9fc8-ac654be83975" :question/math-academy-id "q-95149" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+{e}^{y}\\,j+\\frac{1}{z}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,y={t}^{2},$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\,j+t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\,j+t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a35955fa-8e05-5fc2-9647-6f1fbb5eff50"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b203b6e-7830-50da-aa18-f454f862a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321238} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d8fc-e1b8-5fdc-b9b5-09f8f6941c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321497} {:answer/id #uuid "c175b73f-e2fb-54e8-a7e0-b2906ff65bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322651} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d08b91-916d-5adb-a62d-964ea2cc4e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322710} {:answer/id #uuid "e1542b36-0e72-5814-8c1f-4765946bc3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321497} :answer-field/id #uuid "276fef95-8127-5751-a8f3-63d27774306c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fdba027b-12bc-5b22-aa20-f040a6af0b36" :question/math-academy-id "q-37106" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2{t}^{2}+2$ and $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot 4t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1}{({1}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bb7a2b36-6b63-51f3-9376-761d5f709c35" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131e1312-3232-541a-a772-9a4195876e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484854} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf61e8f-1cbe-5cf7-b7de-cb285c2945df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484868} {:answer/id #uuid "54a8785f-b684-5fbf-ab5e-7a2651308e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485006} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1155ed-ffcf-5482-9835-4e132b93d976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485086} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4e5d76-01d4-5027-9d0b-5f4455a3c94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485006} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f9db34-f1fa-54a6-9839-7f297b6a756c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eabe5e6a-dc85-5118-84d2-9aa9f31a6a23" :question/math-academy-id "q-95086" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+2t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1,$ $y=2t$ and $z={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2t(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)(1+t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bd21bb0d-0ac3-5b0d-8a0d-e84c7b42e305" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485628}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484804}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515625} :activity/id #uuid "362158bd-0c82-5952-946e-c621fd991586" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049255 :topic/id #uuid "e711f285-9c99-50d8-8dee-a4a8b01a7c1a" :topic/math-academy-id 1936} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d91284b-aa86-5efd-8e00-bc13b952dcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321627} {:answer/id #uuid "57674ee3-9965-5502-a818-bbcc31f0235a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321835} {:answer/id #uuid "60879608-43b6-5c2d-8f50-f7877f002d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321904} {:answer/id #uuid "8305ad0c-8f86-50aa-a55a-367d449dabc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322180} {:answer/id #uuid "e18c50bc-afa0-58da-b693-f0e3601201ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322910}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321904} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71b9994-814b-5c24-af5b-f67cb4ffae0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ea55a51-bc91-526a-9277-7689869bc683" :question/math-academy-id "q-36211" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y={e}^{-t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0cdae9f2-99ba-523d-86fd-08256e29cd00" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f3235b-60e2-5c23-a6d1-2912bfc76c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358041} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fdac84-5c94-5bcb-847f-c1a99a8c5411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359347} {:answer/id #uuid "bde855a4-7fc2-52c3-af6f-b2e1fd0d6c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359405} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8b8503-66f4-52dd-b74c-bee16b502323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359689} {:answer/id #uuid "e953648a-9a4f-5892-9fbd-2c82540d2fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359405} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3efa02-f1ae-5df0-98fd-0df55d2c7698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d03333b-22a3-579a-b479-87085056a6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95059" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "386e3776-9252-5f0e-9289-19a65b4311b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445652} {:answer/id #uuid "76ff76d1-a44d-5785-aeb6-f49cd08b8d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445657} {:answer/id #uuid "869d2a2c-eca1-5a81-9c8c-bd38ea652d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445658} {:answer/id #uuid "9980bb90-a6ef-51f4-88b3-4812a2e11e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445662} {:answer/id #uuid "e898d08a-c5cb-5c48-be98-c6dd2a755c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445652} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ea0794-709e-5625-aee2-f32cf13014aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b98d6bad-fbc9-5978-8040-a1a35d995f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-36234" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,2t⟩$ at the point $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a4200b6-903e-5981-924d-9535282a3f9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0618c56b-92f8-5743-9e32-328933507a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}+{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514887} {:answer/id #uuid "42831ed2-f44c-506e-a16d-0e06ead786c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}-{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515014} {:answer/id #uuid "581a6ccb-d929-5a4c-abd5-314717c764d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515068} {:answer/id #uuid "b182ff13-9dbc-5c38-8d5b-05e109460201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515243} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bd0e4e-b413-5783-9897-cec63d3177bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515068} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb54adf-64fe-5825-9ef0-62336c5f858a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2becf284-e142-5ae3-80f6-d208696ea202" :question/math-academy-id "q-95068" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bf608e7a-0173-58ee-a61e-d120aeb92841" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131e1312-3232-541a-a772-9a4195876e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484854} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf61e8f-1cbe-5cf7-b7de-cb285c2945df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484868} {:answer/id #uuid "54a8785f-b684-5fbf-ab5e-7a2651308e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485006} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1155ed-ffcf-5482-9835-4e132b93d976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485086} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4e5d76-01d4-5027-9d0b-5f4455a3c94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485006} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f9db34-f1fa-54a6-9839-7f297b6a756c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eabe5e6a-dc85-5118-84d2-9aa9f31a6a23" :question/math-academy-id "q-95086" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+2t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1,$ $y=2t$ and $z={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2t(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)(1+t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fee8b366-8b24-5d5f-8a6e-56a6d4fdb53d"}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514855}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536972} :activity/id #uuid "a420db12-a105-5bd9-937a-e05193194ef3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049255 :topic/id #uuid "e711f285-9c99-50d8-8dee-a4a8b01a7c1a" :topic/math-academy-id 1936} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ecb641-5c9e-59d2-9af0-e8b4b2b12cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358237} {:answer/id #uuid "3913aa2e-745b-50de-97ac-427a88ec41d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358256} {:answer/id #uuid "58cc3bc9-ecef-54de-9839-9c06a26426b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358521} {:answer/id #uuid "996b9c09-c035-53fe-ba0a-1216f4518389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359098} {:answer/id #uuid "dd14b83a-9e9f-5129-b7b0-19250eeeb3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359098} :answer-field/id #uuid "31bbcbbe-389e-5798-9aef-8032762b891a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "53f0d6f9-0bd0-5fbc-870f-a5ea8fa6cdba" :question/math-academy-id "q-94887" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t},3t-1⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t,y=\\sqrt{t}$ and $z=3t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\sqrt{t}(3t-1),2t(3t-1),2t\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩ \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 2+(6{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+2t\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}+6t\\sqrt{t} \\\\  & =12t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =12(1)\\sqrt{1}-2\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{3(1)^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d9d61a2-d905-5297-b0d3-ec95675b960d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536950}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a36a033-d9b0-5aa7-a7f4-b5451390ac7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445648} {:answer/id #uuid "60dab585-b4f4-59ea-8d98-9a5182b158eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445654} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6d49e7-278c-5501-995f-670dd3f19484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2+2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445660} {:answer/id #uuid "d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445667} {:answer/id #uuid "ff848b94-7fca-53f6-95e5-3ab48c4f01c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445667} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e49b12-6312-563d-bb5a-a9112af44abf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "430b6986-f30c-500a-b5e9-219dd0f3ace5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167463" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,i+xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,j+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=2t$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "664e440a-aede-58bf-9a8e-056fd99ee996"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fb2e01-985f-513d-813b-cfde3f1b377e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358661} {:answer/id #uuid "788c776c-5597-51d2-9394-be9dfb7f75ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358792} {:answer/id #uuid "901c96ff-6cee-55e9-8211-cd0101addfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359007} {:answer/id #uuid "a22be918-009f-5e59-9d70-9b6808c8792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359167} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc10872-0c59-572a-92f2-5c00d7f80870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359542} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef695bf-af69-5590-8e72-7ce89ea59101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ee940918-1cef-5c23-a56c-5afe1494fd57" :question/math-academy-id "q-95146" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,k \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y={e}^{t},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8aab6ae2-020e-5223-9fc4-3a53a2218b2a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65cdcc58-be4b-5338-b00a-ddcb5371bd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358643} {:answer/id #uuid "6b40bd8f-7feb-594d-94b7-c6fe911edb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358691} {:answer/id #uuid "743c9228-cbd8-5669-88ae-262b1ab32094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358757} {:answer/id #uuid "93cbeb11-d6d7-50dd-9acd-c56791d17285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359047} {:answer/id #uuid "bd23f796-bcb5-5c88-998f-bd753a03f298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358691} :answer-field/id #uuid "e85aac1a-a384-579c-bfda-1155a57b6d9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8defdc8d-5773-5ad6-96bf-f8efe589fed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94884" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t-1⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=2t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2{t}^{2},-2(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,2⟩ \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t+(2-4t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-8t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =4(0)^{3}-8(0)+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "97d5963d-f21c-5cdb-abef-05d8b1a46545" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e692ef2-7942-50c0-987c-003e0499c7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479379} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbb788f-4b96-5fa4-a950-81307ab26f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479494} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca40bf8-6377-5d1a-9803-7cbf03f171f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479542} {:answer/id #uuid "c35cedf1-0de5-5562-b74c-d3e0a68630e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479655} {:answer/id #uuid "fc35db42-0a91-517b-8f1b-307df57fe44c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479655} :answer-field/id #uuid "5689c88b-fb4c-5488-beac-e34a9824569a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc2162e8-e3d2-5905-8722-73fb096c219a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94880" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,j$ and $r(t)=({t}^{2}+1)\\,i+t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={t}^{2}+1$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+j)\\cdot (2t\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d8cbd27c-a659-536b-a17d-a2d28ebe3156" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536954}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536279}]}
